"""Edit the research register only; never writes frozen transcription files."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re

R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
p=R/'records/research/root_notes.json'
notes=json.loads(p.read_text())
by={n['id']:n for n in notes}

by['N09']['text']=r'The answer on 009 builds closed sets as arbitrary intersections of members of F_0 and gives the additional minimum-closed-superset condition γ′. Its explicit closure operation is \(A\mapsto\bigcap\{C\in F_0:A\subseteq C\}\). Comparison with the open-test functor C_Y in BISITE D.12 requires a construction relating that operator to the specified category of spaces over Y; no such comparison is supplied here. The separate δ reformulation on 008 is recorded in N81.'
by['N13']['text']=r'The concrete map is \(q:X\to I\), sending a point to its leaf, and the manuscript identifies \(q^{-1}(\overline{\{i\}})\) with \(\overline{X_i}\). An incidence chain \(i_0<\cdots<i_k\) is sent to the corresponding chain of leaf closures, retaining their inclusion maps. This adds an indexing map relevant to the context’s full flag objects; identification with those full objects still requires their additional geometric data. The open-quotient characterization on 014 is recorded separately in N80, while BISITE D.2 and G.10 retain their return-claim status.'
by['N18']['relation']='adds the replacement by a singular-locus criterion and the author’s claimed leafwise consequence'
by['N18']['text']=r'The original requirement that the leaves belong to F_r is cancelled and replaced by a condition on regular loci of saturated closed subsets. The underlined sentence asserts regularity of every leaf as a consequence of this replacement. The explicit two-point calculation in N79 shows that the literal condition S alone does not establish that consequence; stability of the chosen regular class under the relevant restrictions remains a formulation issue. This records the source’s revision without promoting its asserted implication to a proof.'
by['N19']['text']=r'The proof adds singular loci of closures to the recursive family and asserts existence, a least regular partition, finite suprema and a canonical partition. Its use of regular pieces needs the restriction property tested in N79, while the final corollary has no separate proof. The condition’s B/S label and the later d)/f) references remain reading issues, not silently repaired assumptions.'
by['N21']['text']=r'The margin defines \(F_A=\{B\cap A:B\in F\}\) for arbitrary A and asserts a–e, broadening the body’s admissible-A construction. The explicit trace map in N78 produces a family whose relative closure leaves F_A, refuting c) for that literal arbitrary-subset extension. The body’s separate construction for \(A\in F\) remains recorded on its own domain.'
by['N52']['text']=r'The proof combines closedness from properness with openness from equisingularity and connectedness of the target leaf. Its opening says only “partition admissible,” while the continuation invokes equisingularity, connectedness and the manifold condition attached earlier to privileged leaves; those inputs must be tracked explicitly. This is an argument about \(f(F)\subseteq\Phi\), with the stronger simultaneous triviality assertion recorded separately in N82.'
by['N53']['text']=r'The sentence infers that the fibre is a manifold from a local product map between manifold total and base leaves. Those manifold inputs and the selected piecewise morphisms from 030–034 require explicit justification, since the opening remark on 035 says admissible. The unrestricted-topological map in N54 tests the factor inference, with its properness and category qualifications retained.'
by['N54']['text']=r'Bing’s nonmanifold \(B\) has a homeomorphism \(h:B\times\mathbb R\to\mathbb R^4\); the composite \(p=\operatorname{pr}_{\mathbb R}\circ h^{-1}:\mathbb R^4\to\mathbb R\) is a globally trivial bundle with fibre \(B\) [Bing58,Bing59]. This explicitly refutes the bare inference from manifold total and base spaces to manifold fibres through that projection. Our diagnostic has high confidence from the cited construction; application against the full manuscript statement additionally requires the proper-map premise of 035, membership in its selected piecewise category, and, for 028, the specified mobility values. None of those further qualifications is claimed for this example.'
by['N55']['text']=r'The recursion begins with the largest regular open U of the base over which \(X|_U\to U\) is locally trivial, and uses canonical partitions of fibres over U. The paragraph attributes the intrinsic immobile-point construction to the first letter to A-Campo, whose exact identification remains a source task. Its asserted compatibility with products and transition maps is recorded separately in N83, alongside the explicit conjectural status of the recursion.'
by['N58']['text']=r'The author enlarges the inductive input from a bare map to a map with finitely many marked closed subspaces and their intersections. He then says the intrinsic construction is “probablement” obtained by paraphrasing the first A-Campo letter. This records a method still to be completed; N84 isolates the necessary finiteness check for the new family introduced during recursion.'
by['N59']['text']=r'The datum \(\alpha:p^*\mathcal F\to i^*\mathcal G\) is sent by \(p^*\dashv p_*\) to \(\mathcal F\to p_*i^*\mathcal G\), using the unit followed by \(p_*\alpha\); the inverse sends \(\beta\) to the counit composed with \(p^*\beta\). The two adjunction identities prove that these are inverse mate correspondences in the chosen coefficient category. Comparison with STRATA C.6 additionally requires a specified crossing span, an identification of its \(t_*s^*\) with this \(p_*i^*\), and compatible coefficient functors. The page has not specified a higher coefficient enhancement, so no such enhancement is inserted into this calculation.'
by['N62']['text']=r'The page stipulates \(V_i^\circ\) and boundary pieces \(\partial_jV_i^\circ\) fibred over \(V_j^\circ\). The boundary of \(V_i^\circ\) is empty for minimal i; for a predecessor j of i, the fibres of \(\partial_jV_i^\circ\to V_j^\circ\) have empty boundary, so the corresponding relative fibre-boundary piece vanishes. Those actual fibrations are the comparison data alongside the context’s normal-crossing models; a general equivalence of the constructions is not established by this page.'
by['N66']['text']=r'The recipe builds \(V=V^\circ\amalg_{U\prime}U\) and then \(W=W^\circ\amalg_{V\prime}V\), with attachment maps that collapse the specified boundary fibres onto the lower pieces. Comparison therefore retains \(V\prime\to V\) and \(U\prime\to U\) together with the inclusions into the upper pieces. The preceding regularity assertion for the intermediate gluing V′ is isolated in N85; existence, uniqueness and recovery of the whole equisingular structure have no supplied proof on 044.'
by['N69']['text']=r'The comparison task is to choose a normal-crossing boundary realization and write the domain, codomain and formula of each flag map, then compare these with the projections and squares on 047. This note has not supplied a map from that realization to the manuscript’s \(V_i^\circ\), or compatibility with the punctured normal-line bundles of 147 and arithmetic tori \(T^J\) of 149. This is our proposed source-comparison task, with medium confidence in its relevance because the cited pages explicitly carry boundary and normal-bundle data; the required maps, global branch permutations and twisting remain work to perform.'

def add(id,scans,kind,anchor,context,relation,register,text,terms):
 by[id]=dict(id=id,scans=scans,kind=kind,anchor=anchor,context=context,relation=relation,register=register,text=text,search_terms=terms)

C147='MPL_147_138-190_transcription.tex'
add('N78','019 margin, arbitrary traces','own idea — explicit counterexample','une partie quelconque A de X',f'{C147}, scans 182–184; N20–N21','adds an explicit failure of c) for the marginal extension','WG2 B15 (§1.2) [unchecked plan]; no directly settling entry',r'Take \(X=\mathbb R^2\), \(L=\mathbb R\times\{0\}\), and \(F=\{\varnothing,L,X\setminus L,X\}\): this is Boolean and closure-stable, each member has dense relative interior in its closure, and its closed rare chains have length at most two, verifying a–e. Put \(P=\{(0,1/n):n\geq1\}\), \(A=L\cup P\), and apply the trace map \(B\mapsto B\cap A\), giving \(F_A=\{\varnothing,L,P,A\}\). The relative closure is \(\overline P^{\,A}=P\cup\{(0,0)\}\), which is none of those four sets, so c) fails for this explicit arbitrary trace. Confidence is high because the four members and every relevant closure are enumerated; this tests the margin’s literal claim and leaves the body’s \(A\in F\) argument on its own domain.', ['partie','fermé','local'])
add('N79','017–018, regularity implication','own idea — explicit counterexample','Ceci implique que les feuilles sont ∈F_r',f'{C147}, scans 181–182; N18–N19','adds a finite test of the claimed implication from the written S criterion','INTAKE 20261114 §13.4 [intake lead]; no directly settling entry',r'Let \(X=\{0,1\}\) have closed sets \(\varnothing,\{0\},X\), take \(F=\mathcal P(X)\), and include \(F_r=\{\varnothing,\{0\},X\}\) into F. Every closed Y has \(Y_{\rm reg}=Y\), so the written S criterion holds, and the singleton partition has \(F_{\mathcal P}=F\) with every singular locus empty and therefore saturated. Its leaf \(\{1\}\) is absent from F_r, directly disproving the stated leafwise implication under these literal data, although all members of F_r are locally closed and F satisfies a–f. Confidence is high because the finite topologies and families are explicit; the example identifies the missing restriction-stability issue without changing the frozen definition.', ['réguli','strate','local'])
add('N80','014, quotient characterization','unproved claim','application ouverte',f'{C147}, scans 185–188; N13','adds the open-quotient assertion beyond the singleton closure formula','BISITE D.2 and G.10 [return claims]; WG2 B15 (§1.2) [unchecked plan]',r'The page asserts that \(q:X\to I\) is open and characterizes finite cellular decompositions through finite présobre quotients with open quotient map. Verification requires the claimed closure-commutation identity on arbitrary subsets of I as well as on singletons. The indexing map in N13 supplies the concrete comparison with incidence chains; the full geometric flag objects of 147 require their own additional data.', ['quotient','ouvert','adhérence'])
add('N81','008, δ reformulation','unproved claim / reformulation','δ',f'{C147}, scans 181–184; N06 and N09','adds the proposed equivalence of termination formulations','WG2 B15 (§1.2) [unchecked plan]',r'The page reformulates e) using decreasing chains of members of F and the stated F_0-rarity condition. The verification must track the operation sending such a chain to the closed chain used in e), and the converse use of the same rarity convention. This is separate from the later coarsest-topology construction and is recorded without silently identifying the two sorts of chains.', ['rare','suite','fermé'])
add('N82','036, whole inverse image with its partition','unproved claim','avec sa partition induite',f'{C147}, scans 181–182,190; N52–N53','adds a simultaneous triviality assertion with explicit chart requirement','INTAKE 20261114 §13.4 [intake lead]; RESIDUE G.5 [return claim]',r'The continuation asserts local triviality of the whole \(f^{-1}(\Phi)\) together with its induced partition. The required datum is one chart \(f^{-1}(O)\cong O\times K\) over an open \(O\subseteq\Phi\), carrying every induced leaf to \(O\times K_F\). The invoked connectedness and privileged manifold conditions from the preceding discussion must accompany this assertion, whose simultaneous chart requirement is stronger data than separate fibre descriptions.', ['localement','partition','fibré'])
add('N83','037, canonical partition over the trivial locus','unproved claim','partition canonique intrinsèque',f'{C147}, scans 181–182,190; N55','adds compatibility obligations for transporting intrinsic partitions','INTAKE 20261114 §13.4 [intake lead]; WG H4 [unchecked hunch]',r'The construction identifies the intrinsic canonical partition over the trivial locus with products of base opens and canonical fibre leaves. Its use requires compatibility with products and with transition maps between the chosen trivializations. Those maps are the comparison data needed to make the resulting partition independent of the local presentation; the text supplies no separate verification here.', ['canonique','produit','local'])
add('N84','037, finite marked data in the induction','unproved claim','une famille finie',f'{C147}, scans 153–155; N57–N58','adds a finiteness obligation in the strengthened recursion','INTAKE 20261114 §13.4 [intake lead]; STRATA C.5 [return claim]',r'The recursive problem accepts finitely many marked closed subsets, while the construction introduces the family \(\{\Phi_i\prime\}\) from closures of leaves over U. Its finiteness is therefore an input still requiring justification before applying that inductive statement. The notation discrepancy between the body and margin remains the separate reading question in N57.', ['fini','fermé','admissible'])
add('N85','044, intermediate boundary gluing','unproved claim','régulier',f'{C147}, scans 170–172; N66–N67','adds the regularity claim preceding the two reconstruction quotients','STRATA C.3,C.6 [return claims]; WG H4 [unchecked hunch]',r'Before the two quotient constructions, the page asserts regularity of the intermediate gluing \(V\prime=V^{\circ\prime}\amalg_{\partial V^{\circ\prime}=\partial U\prime\prime}U\prime\prime\). Verification requires the actual boundary identification and the permitted gluing construction in the selected category. N67 retains the discrepancy between the literal item 6 and the adjacent diagram, so this note’s displayed expression identifies the diagrammatic gluing being tested without correcting that line.', ['recollement','réguli','bord'])

notes=[by[k] for k in sorted(by,key=lambda s:int(s[1:]))]
# Typographical changes here concern the English notes only.
for n in notes:
 for k in ('scans','context','relation','register','text','anchor'):
  n[k]=re.sub(r'\b(on|in|and|whereas|intermediate|cancelled|faint|just as|with|scan|cote)(?=\d)',r'\1 ',n[k])
  n[k]=re.sub(r'(?<=\d),(?=\d)',', ',n[k])
 for old,new in [('from1 and2','from 1 and 2'),('condition2','condition 2'),('itema)','item a)'),('PropositionA','Proposition A'),('PropositionB','Proposition B'),('PropositionC','Proposition C'),('PropositionD','Proposition D'),('Conjecture1','Conjecture 1'),('Conjecture2','Conjecture 2'),('item6','item 6'),('at 024 as e_n','at 024 as e_n')]:
  n['scans']=n['scans'].replace(old,new)
  n['text']=n['text'].replace(old,new)
 n['text']=re.sub(r'(?<!\\)overline F',r'\\(\\overline F\\)',n['text'])
 n['text']=re.sub(r'(?<!\\)underline F',r'\\(\\underline F\\)',n['text'])
 n['anchor']=re.sub(r'(?<!\\)overline F',r'\\(\\overline F\\)',n['anchor'])
 n['anchor']=n['anchor'].replace('dot A',r'\(\dot A\)')
 for old,new in [(r'U\prime\prime',"U''"),(r'U\prime',"U'"),(r'V\prime',"V'"),(r'\Phi_i\prime',r"\Phi'_i")]:
  n['text']=n['text'].replace(old,new)
p.write_text(json.dumps(notes,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
out=['## Page-by-page research register\n\nEach item is separate from the frozen transcription. Context transcriptions retain their supplied first-pass status; questions and retractions remain recorded even where a later argument or an explicit check answers them. Stable item identifiers are retained, with entries grouped by their first source scan.\n']
for n in sorted(notes,key=lambda x:(int(re.search(r'\d+',x['scans']).group()),int(x['id'][1:]))):
 out.append(f"### {n['id']} — cote 121, scans {n['scans']}\n\n**Kind:** {n['kind']}. **Anchor:** “{n['anchor']}”.\n\n**Context:** {n['context']}. **Relationship:** {n['relation']}. **Register:** {n['register']}.\n\n{n['text']}\n")
(R/'records/research/root_notes.md').write_text('\n'.join(out))

ctxparts=[]
for f in (R.parent/'context').glob('*transcription.tex'):
 parts=re.split(r'\\scanpage\{(\d+)\}',f.read_text())
 for i in range(1,len(parts),2):ctxparts.append((f.name,int(parts[i]),parts[i+1]))
audit=[]
for n in notes:
 hits=[]
 for fn,s,b in ctxparts:
  terms=[t for t in n['search_terms'] if re.search(re.escape(t),b,re.I)]
  if terms:hits.append(dict(file=fn,scan=s,terms=terms))
 audit.append(dict(note=n['id'],terms=n['search_terms'],hits=hits))
(R/'records/research/context_search_audit.json').write_text(json.dumps(audit,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print(f'Finalized {len(notes)} research items and their context searches; frozen files not opened for writing.')
